# BAR Lobby Design System Rules

## Core Principle
**Always prefer the design system. Use reusable components/utilities over hard-coded styles or bespoke solutions. Break the rules intentionally, not accidentally.**

## Design System Components

### Required Components (prefer these over custom solutions):
- **Buttons**: Always use `<Button>` from `@renderer/components/controls/Button.vue`
  - Use classes: `green`, `red`, `blue`, `black`, `grey`, `slim`, `large`, `fullwidth`
  - Avoid creating custom button elements or styles
  
- **Panels**: Always use `<Panel>` from `@renderer/components/common/Panel.vue`
  - Use `no-padding` prop when needed
  - Avoid creating custom panel/container divs with backgrounds/borders
  
- **Interactive Tiles**: Use `<InteractiveTile>` from `@renderer/components/common/InteractiveTile.vue`
  - Supports variants: `saturate`, `selected`, `readMoreVariant`
  - Use slots: `#media`, `#content`, `#body`, `#button`
  
- **Other Components**: 
  - `<Loader>`, `<Modal>`, `<StatusCard>`, `<ScrollingTextPanel>`, `<Divider>`
  - `<Select>`, `<Checkbox>`, `<ToggleSwitch>`, `<Textbox>`, `<Number>`, `<SearchBox>`
  - Check `src/renderer/components/common/` and `src/renderer/components/controls/` before creating custom UI

## Spacing System

### ALWAYS prefer spacing utilities - avoid hard-coding padding/margin/gap values:

**Available spacing scale:**
- `xxs`: 2px
- `xs`: 4px  
- `sm`: 8px
- `md`: 12px
- `lg`: 16px
- `xl`: 24px
- `xxl`: 32px
- `xxxl`: 48px
- `xxxxl`: 56px

**Usage:**
```vue
<!-- ✅ PREFERRED -->
<div class="flex-col gap-lg padding-xl">
<div class="padding-top-md padding-left-xxl">
<div class="margin-bottom-lg">

<!-- ⚠️ AVOID (unless intentional) -->
<div style="padding: 16px; gap: 24px;">
<div class="custom-padding"> /* with padding: 12px in CSS */
```

**Rules:**
- Use `gap-*` for flex/grid gaps
- Use `padding-*` or `padding-{direction}-*` for padding
- Use `margin-*` or `margin-{direction}-*` for margins
- Use `map-get($spacing, "key")` in SCSS when needed
- **Avoid** writing `padding: 16px`, `margin: 8px`, etc. directly unless you have a specific reason

## Typography System

### ALWAYS prefer typography classes - avoid hard-coding font sizes/weights:

**Available typography classes:**
- **Titles**: `.title-1`, `.title-2`, `.title-3`, `.large-title`, `.display`
- **Subtitles**: `.subtitle-1`, `.subtitle-2`, `.subtitle-2-stronger`
- **Body**: `.body-1`, `.body-1-strong`, `.body-2`, `.body-2-strong`, `.body-2-stronger`
- **Caption**: `.caption-1`, `.caption-1-strong`, `.caption-1-stronger`, `.caption-2`, `.caption-2-strong`
- **Semantic HTML**: `h1`, `h2`, `h3`, `h4`, `h5`, `h6`, `p` (use these with typography classes)

**Usage:**
```vue
<!-- ✅ PREFERRED -->
<h2 class="title-2">Heading</h2>
<p class="body-1">Body text</p>
<div class="subtitle-1">Subtitle</div>

<!-- ⚠️ AVOID (unless intentional) -->
<div style="font-size: 20px; font-weight: 600;">
<div class="custom-text"> /* with font-size: 16px in CSS */
```

**Rules:**
- Always apply typography classes to text elements
- Use semantic HTML (`h1-h6`, `p`) with typography classes
- **Avoid** writing `font-size: 16px`, `font-weight: 600`, etc. directly unless you have a specific reason

## Layout Utilities

### Use existing layout utilities:
- `flex-row`, `flex-col` for flexbox
- `fullwidth`, `fullheight` for sizing
- `flex-grow`, `flex-shrink`, `flex-center-items` for flex behavior
- `gap-*` utilities (see spacing system)

## Guidelines (Not Hard Rules)

### ⚠️ Avoid `!important` unless intentional
- **Avoid** using `!important` in CSS/SCSS
- If you need to override styles, fix the specificity issue properly first
- Use design system components/utilities instead of fighting with CSS
- If you find yourself needing `!important`, reconsider your approach - can the design system help?
- **However**: If you have a legitimate, intentional reason to use `!important`, document why in a comment

### ⚠️ Avoid hard-coding styles when design system exists
- Prefer design system utilities over inline styles for spacing, typography, colors (unless truly dynamic)
- Avoid custom CSS classes that duplicate design system functionality
- Avoid bespoke components that recreate existing design system components
- **However**: If you have a specific, intentional reason to break this, document it

### ⚠️ Avoid one-off solutions
- Check if a design system component/utility exists first
- If it doesn't exist but could be reusable, create it as a design system component
- Only create truly unique, non-reusable code when absolutely necessary
- **However**: Sometimes one-offs are needed - just make sure it's intentional

## Code Review Checklist

Before writing any code, ask:
1. ✅ Is there a design system component for this? (Check `components/common/` and `components/controls/`)
2. ✅ Am I using spacing utilities instead of hard-coded values?
3. ✅ Am I using typography classes instead of hard-coded font styles?
4. ✅ Am I avoiding `!important` (or documenting why I need it)?
5. ✅ Is this code reusable, or should it be a design system component?
6. ✅ If I'm breaking a rule, is it intentional and documented?

## Examples

### ✅ Preferred Code:
```vue
<template>
  <Panel class="flex-col gap-lg" no-padding>
    <h2 class="title-2 padding-left-xxl padding-top-xxl">Title</h2>
    <div class="flex-row gap-md padding-xl">
      <Button class="green">Action</Button>
      <Button>Cancel</Button>
    </div>
    <p class="body-1 padding-left-xxl">Description text</p>
  </Panel>
</template>
```

### ⚠️ Code to Avoid (unless intentional):
```vue
<template>
  <div class="custom-panel">
    <div class="title" style="font-size: 28px; padding: 32px 0 0 32px;">Title</div>
    <div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; padding: 24px;">
      <button class="custom-button">Action</button>
    </div>
    <p style="font-size: 16px; margin-left: 32px;">Description</p>
  </div>
</template>

<style>
.custom-panel {
  background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  padding: 16px !important; /* ⚠️ Avoid unless intentional - document why */
}
</style>
```

## When to Extend the Design System

If you need something that doesn't exist:

1. **Check if it can be achieved with existing components/props**
   - Review existing components for props/variants that might work
   - Consider composition of existing components

2. **If not, create a new design system component (not a one-off)**
   - Make it reusable and configurable via props
   - Follow existing component patterns and structure

3. **Add it to the appropriate directory**
   - `components/common/` for general UI components
   - `components/controls/` for form/input components

4. **Document it in the design system**
   - Add examples to the styles showcase pages
   - Document props, slots, and usage patterns

5. **Make it reusable and configurable via props**
   - Avoid hard-coded values
   - Use design system spacing/typography internally
   - Support common use cases through props

**Remember: The design system exists to prevent code duplication and maintain consistency. Use it! But if you need to break the rules, do it intentionally and document why.**

